WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.440
In a documentary about the making of&nbsp;
Avatar, director James Cameron revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.580
that the original design for the Na'vi&nbsp;
was much less graceful and feline than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:13.260
the aliens we see on screen. Because Avatar&nbsp;
is a love and assimilation story where Jake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:17.400
Sully falls for Neytiri and becomes one of&nbsp;
the Na'vi, the character designers opted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.080
to make the Na'vi humanoid and exotically&nbsp;
beautiful. In the documentary, Cameron said,

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.660
"I wanted people to say, 'I&nbsp;
want to be one of them.'"

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.100
Cameron told Gizmodo that he relied on feedback&nbsp;
from his crew of artists for this reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.000
adjusting the design until they felt it was&nbsp;
plausible that a human like Jake could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.560
attracted to one. Cameron also said that&nbsp;
they emphasized the alien and the animal a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:41.520
bit more in the older Na'vi characters to make&nbsp;
meeting the future in-laws more frightening.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.020
In an interview with Playboy, Cameron explained&nbsp;
how he designed Neytiri to appeal to his male&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:50.880
audience's id. He explained that the heterosexual&nbsp;
male perception of female beauty was extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.560
unrealistic, and he didn't expect male viewers&nbsp;
to be grossed out by the Na'vi in light of this.

